Java 8 (2014)

Java 8 was a major release that introduced several foundational features that paved the way for functional programming in Java and greatly improved the language's expressiveness and productivity.

Key Features of Java 8

1. Lambda Expressions

    Enables functional programming by allowing you to treat functions as first-class citizens.
    Example:

     java
     List<String> list = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
     list.forEach(name -> System.out.println(name));
     

2. Streams API

    Provides a declarative way to process collections, supporting operations such as filtering, mapping, and reducing.
    Example:

     java
     List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
     int sum = numbers.stream()
                      .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
                      .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)
                      .sum();
     

3. Default and Static Methods in Interfaces

    Allows interfaces to include default method implementations, reducing the need for abstract classes.
    Example:

     java
     interface MyInterface {
         default void print() {
             System.out.println("Default implementation");
         }
         static void staticMethod() {
             System.out.println("Static method in interface");
         }
     }
     

4. Optional

    Provides a container object which may or may not contain a non-null value, helping to avoid null pointer exceptions.
    Example:

     java
     Optional<String> optional = Optional.of("Java8");
     optional.ifPresent(System.out::println);
     

5. New Date and Time API (java.time package)

    Introduces a modern and comprehensive API for date and time manipulation.
    Example:

     java
     LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();
     LocalDate tomorrow = today.plusDays(1);
     System.out.println("Tomorrow is: " + tomorrow);
     

6. Method References

    Offers a shorthand notation for calling methods via lambda expressions.
    Example:

     java
     List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
     names.forEach(System.out::println);
     

7. Nashorn JavaScript Engine

    Integrates a lightweight JavaScript runtime, allowing Java applications to execute JavaScript code.
    Example:

     java
     ScriptEngine engine = new ScriptEngineManager().getEngineByName("nashorn");
     engine.eval("print('Hello from Nashorn');");
     

8. Repeating Annotations

    Permits the use of the same annotation more than once on the same declaration.
    Example:

     java
     @Schedule(dayOfWeek = "Monday")
     @Schedule(dayOfWeek = "Wednesday")
     public class MyScheduler { 
         // Class implementation 
     }
     

Java 9 to 17 Overview

While Java 8 laid the groundwork with functional programming and improved APIs, subsequent releases introduced additional features and improvements:

Java 9 (2017)

 Module System (Project Jigsaw): Enhances encapsulation and modularity.
 JShell (REPL): Interactive tool for trying out Java code snippets.
 Factory Methods for Collections: `List.of()`, `Set.of()`, and `Map.of()`.
 Stream API Enhancements: New methods like `dropWhile()`, `takeWhile()`, and `ofNullable()`.
 Private Methods in Interfaces: Allow interfaces to have helper methods.

Java 10 (2018)

 Local Variable Type Inference (`var`): Reduces boilerplate in variable declarations.
 Unmodifiable Collections (`copyOf()`): Makes it easier to create immutable collections.

Java 11 (2018) – LTS

 String Enhancements: Methods like `isBlank()`, `lines()`, and `strip()`.
 New `HttpClient` API: A modern HTTP client for making network calls.
 Run Java Files Directly: Simplifies testing of small Java programs.

Java 12 to Java 17

 Java 12 (2019): Introduced a preview of Switch Expressions and enhancements to the Collectors API.
 Java 13 (2019): Previewed Text Blocks for easier multi-line string creation; refined switch expressions.
 Java 14 (2020): Previewed Records (a compact syntax for data classes) and Pattern Matching for `instanceof`.
 Java 15 (2020): Introduced Sealed Classes (preview) and Hidden Classes for dynamic code generation.
 Java 16 (2021): Finalized Pattern Matching for `instanceof` and Records; added new collection methods.
 Java 17 (2021) – LTS: Finalized Sealed Classes and introduced Pattern Matching for Switch (as a preview), among other improvements.

Summary

 Java 8 introduced transformational features like Lambda Expressions, Streams API, and a new Date and Time API.
 Subsequent versions (9–17) have continued to evolve the language with features for better modularity, improved performance, and more concise syntax.

Each release builds upon the previous one, making modern Java both powerful and flexible for various types of applications. If you have any questions about a specific feature or need more detailed examples, feel free to ask!

