Java 18 (March 2022)

 Simple Web Server (Preview):
  A minimal command-line tool to serve static files from a directory, useful for testing or sharing local content quickly.
 UTF-8 as the Default Charset:
  Standardizes UTF-8 as the default charset across all platforms, improving consistency in text handling.
 Code Snippets in API Documentation (Preview):
  Enhances Java’s API documentation by allowing embedded code snippets that can be automatically tested.

Java 19 (September 2022)

 Virtual Threads (Preview):
  Introduced as part of Project Loom, virtual threads provide lightweight concurrency, making it easier to write, maintain, and scale concurrent applications.
 Structured Concurrency (Preview):
  Simplifies the management of multiple tasks running in parallel by introducing a structured approach to thread management, error handling, and cancellation.
 Enhancements to Pattern Matching & Records:
  Continued refinements to the language’s pattern matching capabilities and record types, building on earlier previews.

Java 20 (March 2023)

 Record Patterns (Second Preview):
  Further enhances the pattern matching capability by allowing deconstruction of record values, making it easier to work with data carriers.
 Pattern Matching for Switch (Third Preview):
  Improves the expressiveness and safety of switch expressions/statements by allowing patterns as case labels.
 Scoped Values (Incubator):
  Introduces a new mechanism for safely sharing immutable data between threads, aiming to provide an alternative to thread-local variables.
 Foreign Function & Memory API (Second Incubator):
  Continues to refine the API that allows Java programs to interoperate with code and data outside of the JVM, improving performance and safety.

Java 21 (September 2023 – LTS)

 Virtual Threads:
  In this LTS release, virtual threads continue to mature (often still as a preview), providing a major leap forward for concurrent programming by significantly reducing the cost of thread creation.
 Pattern Matching Enhancements:
  Some pattern matching features (like for switch expressions) may have been finalized or continue to be refined, simplifying conditional logic.
 Structured Concurrency & Incubating APIs:
  Further evolution of structured concurrency, scoped values, and the Foreign Function & Memory API—enabling more robust and efficient multi-threaded and low-level programming.
 General Performance & Stability Improvements:
  As an LTS release, Java 21 also focuses on long-term stability, security, and performance, making it a solid choice for enterprise applications.

Java 22 and Java 23 (March 2024 & September 2024)

 Incremental Enhancements:
  These releases have largely focused on maturing the preview and incubator features introduced in previous versions. Expect:

   Continued refinements to virtual threads and structured concurrency.
   Further stabilization and possible finalization of pattern matching for switch and record patterns.
   Enhancements to the Foreign Function & Memory API and scoped values, increasing performance and usability.
 Performance and Security Updates:
  Incremental performance tuning, improved garbage collection, and security updates are typical in these releases.

Note: With the rapid release cadence, many new features are introduced first as preview or incubator features before they are finalized. This means that while the core concepts remain consistent, the APIs may undergo minor changes as they mature.

